// GENERATED by scripts/build-courses.ts from src/lib/courses/content — edit those files, then run: npm run courses
import type { LessonText } from '../types';
const text: LessonText[][] = [[{"say":"AP Precalculus is all about how quantities change together. Rates of change are the main lens for every function family.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The average rate of change of f on [a, b] is (f(b) − f(a))/(b − a), the slope of the secant line."},{"t":"key","text":"A function is increasing where its outputs rise as inputs rise. It's concave up when its rate of change is increasing, and concave down when its rate of change is decreasing."},{"t":"p","text":"A point of inflection is where concavity changes, which is where the rate of change switches from increasing to decreasing (or vice versa)."},{"t":"ex","problem":"f(1) = 3, f(2) = 7, f(3) = 15. Is f concave up or down on [1, 3]?","steps":["Rate on [1, 2]: 4. Rate on [2, 3]: 8","The rate of change is increasing"],"answer":"Concave up"},{"t":"p","text":"Linear functions have a constant rate of change; quadratic functions have a rate of change that changes at a constant rate."},{"t":"lab","kind":"demo","title":"Average speed over shrinking intervals","aim":"A ball rolls so that its distance is d(t) = t². Find its average speed from t = 2 to t = 2 + h as h gets smaller.","sets":[{"name":"h","start":1,"label":"Interval length h (s)","min":0.01,"max":2,"step":0.01}],"outs":[{"name":"avg","expr":"((2 + h)^2 - 4)/h","label":"Average rate of change (m/s)"}],"steps":["Shrink h toward 0. What value does the average rate approach?"],"noise":0,"result":"The average rate of change over [2, 2 + h] is 4 + h. As the interval shrinks it approaches 4 m/s, the instantaneous rate of change at t = 2.","plot":{"x":"h","y":"avg"}}]},{"say":"Polynomials are built from powers of x. Their zeros, degree and leading term tell the whole story.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A polynomial of degree n has at most n real zeros and at most n − 1 extrema. Complex zeros of real polynomials come in conjugate pairs."},{"t":"key","text":"End behavior is controlled by the leading term: as x → ±∞, the polynomial acts like its highest-degree term."},{"t":"list","items":["Even degree: both ends go the same way. Odd degree: ends go opposite ways.","A zero of multiplicity 2 touches the x-axis; multiplicity 1 or 3 crosses it.","Even functions are symmetric about the y-axis; odd functions about the origin."]},{"t":"ex","problem":"Describe the end behavior of p(x) = −2x⁵ + 3x² − 1.","steps":["Odd degree, negative leading coefficient"],"answer":"As x → ∞, p(x) → −∞; as x → −∞, p(x) → ∞"}]},{"say":"Rational functions model situations like averages and concentrations. Their asymptotes describe limiting behavior.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"For a rational function r(x) = p(x)/q(x), compare degrees to find end behavior: horizontal asymptote y = 0 (bottom bigger), y = ratio of leading coefficients (equal), or slant/no horizontal asymptote (top bigger)."},{"t":"p","text":"Vertical asymptotes occur at zeros of the denominator that don't cancel; holes occur where factors cancel. Use limit notation: as x → 3⁺, r(x) → ∞."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A company's average cost per item is C(x) = (500 + 2x)/x. What happens as production grows?","steps":["Degrees are equal: leading coefficients 2 and 1"],"answer":"C(x) → 2: the average cost approaches $2 per item"},{"t":"p","text":"Function models are chosen by looking at data patterns, then checked with residuals. Piecewise functions model situations with different rules on different intervals."}]}],[{"say":"Arithmetic sequences grow by adding; geometric sequences grow by multiplying. These are the building blocks of linear and exponential functions.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Arithmetic sequences correspond to linear functions (constant differences). Geometric sequences correspond to exponential functions (constant ratios)."},{"t":"list","items":["Arithmetic: aₙ = a₀ + dn. Geometric: gₙ = g₀rⁿ.","Exponential function: f(x) = abˣ, with initial value a and base b. Over equal-length input intervals, outputs change by equal proportions."]},{"t":"ex","problem":"Data: x = 0, 1, 2, 3 and y = 5, 15, 45, 135. Which model fits?","steps":["Ratios: 15/5 = 3, 45/15 = 3, 135/45 = 3"],"answer":"Exponential, y = 5(3)ˣ"},{"t":"p","text":"Exponential growth always eventually exceeds any polynomial growth."}]},{"say":"Logarithms are the inverses of exponential functions. They turn multiplication into addition and powers into products.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"log_b(c) = a means bᵃ = c. The logarithm of a value tells you the exponent."},{"t":"p","text":"Properties: log_b(xy) = log_b x + log_b y; log_b(x/y) = log_b x − log_b y; log_b(xⁿ) = n log_b x; change of base log_b x = ln x / ln b."},{"t":"p","text":"The graph of y = log_b x passes through (1, 0) and has a vertical asymptote at x = 0. Over equal-length output intervals, inputs change proportionally."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Solve 4·2ˣ = 48.","steps":["2ˣ = 12","x = log₂ 12 = ln 12 / ln 2"],"answer":"x ≈ 3.585"}]},{"say":"Exponential data looks curved on a normal graph, but straight on a semi-log plot. That's a powerful modeling trick.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"If data is exponential, plotting ln(y) against x gives a straight line. The slope of that line is ln b."},{"t":"p","text":"On a semi-log plot, the vertical axis is logarithmic (each tick multiplies by a constant factor). Linear patterns on a semi-log plot indicate exponential relationships."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A semi-log plot of ln(y) vs. x gives the line ln(y) = 0.7x + 2. Write the exponential model.","steps":["y = e^(0.7x + 2) = e²·e^(0.7x)"],"answer":"y ≈ 7.39(2.01)ˣ"},{"t":"p","text":"Models are built from data by regression (exponential, logarithmic, power). Choose a model based on the context and on residual plots that show no pattern."},{"t":"lab","kind":"demo","title":"Bacteria on a semi-log plot","aim":"A bacteria culture starts at 100 cells and doubles every 3 hours. Compare the count with its logarithm.","sets":[{"name":"t","start":6,"label":"Time (hours)","min":0,"max":30,"step":1},{"name":"dbl","start":3,"label":"Doubling time (hours)","min":1,"max":6,"step":0.5}],"outs":[{"name":"N","expr":"100*2^(t/dbl)","label":"Bacteria"},{"name":"logN","expr":"log(N)","label":"log₁₀(bacteria)"}],"steps":["Is the log₁₀ graph straight? What is its slope?"],"noise":0,"result":"Exponential data look linear on a semi-log plot because log N = log 100 + (log 2/d)t. The slope reveals the growth rate, which is how scientists spot exponential growth.","plot":{"x":"t","y":"logN"}}]}],[{"say":"Many things repeat: tides, seasons, sound waves. Trigonometric functions model these periodic phenomena.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A periodic function repeats its values over an interval called the period. Sine and cosine come from the unit circle: for an angle θ in standard position, the terminal ray meets the circle at (cos θ, sin θ)."},{"t":"key","text":"Angles are measured in radians: a full rotation is 2π. Key values come from special triangles (π/6, π/4, π/3)."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Find sin(5π/6) and cos(5π/6).","steps":["5π/6 is in Quadrant II with reference angle π/6"],"answer":"sin = 1/2, cos = −√3/2"}]},{"say":"Sinusoidal functions model anything that cycles smoothly. Four parameters control their shape.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"f(θ) = a sin(b(θ + c)) + d: amplitude |a|, period 2π/|b|, phase shift −c, midline y = d."},{"t":"p","text":"To build a model from data: midline = (max + min)/2, amplitude = (max − min)/2, period from the time between peaks, then choose sine or cosine based on where the cycle starts."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A Ferris wheel's seat ranges from 2 m to 42 m high and completes a rotation every 40 s, starting at the bottom. Model its height.","steps":["Midline 22, amplitude 20, period 40 so b = 2π/40 = π/20","Starting at the minimum: use −cos"],"answer":"h(t) = −20 cos(πt/20) + 22"},{"t":"lab","kind":"demo","title":"Tides in a harbor","aim":"Water depth in a harbor rises and falls with a period of about 12.4 hours. Model it with a sinusoid.","sets":[{"name":"t","start":6,"label":"Hours after midnight","min":0,"max":24,"step":0.25},{"name":"A","start":1.2,"label":"Tidal range ÷ 2 (m)","min":0.3,"max":3,"step":0.1}],"outs":[{"name":"h","expr":"3 + A*sin(2*pi*(t - 3)/12.4)","label":"Water depth (m)"}],"steps":["Identify the midline, amplitude and period. When is it high tide?"],"noise":0,"result":"h = 3 + A sin(2π(t − 3)/12.4): midline 3 m, amplitude A, period 12.4 h, phase shift 3 h. A boat needing 3.5 m can only enter around high tide.","plot":{"x":"t","y":"h"}}]},{"say":"Tangent has a different shape, inverse trig functions find angles, and polar functions draw curves by angle and distance.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Tangent: tan θ = sin θ / cos θ, the slope of the terminal ray. It has period π and vertical asymptotes where cos θ = 0."},{"t":"p","text":"Inverse trig functions output angles on restricted domains: arcsin and arctan output in [−π/2, π/2]; arccos in [0, π]."},{"t":"key","text":"Polar functions r = f(θ) give the distance from the origin as the angle changes. If r is increasing (and positive), points move away from the origin; if r is decreasing, they move toward it."},{"t":"ex","problem":"For r = 3 + 2cos θ, what are the maximum and minimum distances from the origin?","steps":["cos θ ranges from −1 to 1"],"answer":"Maximum 5 (at θ = 0), minimum 1 (at θ = π)"}]}],[{"say":"Parametric functions describe motion: where something is at each moment. Vectors describe its direction and speed.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Parametric functions give x(t) and y(t). The average rate of change of each component tells how fast the position changes horizontally and vertically."},{"t":"key","text":"A vector ⟨a, b⟩ has magnitude √(a² + b²). The dot product u · v = u₁v₁ + u₂v₂ is zero for perpendicular vectors."},{"t":"p","text":"A particle's velocity can be described by a vector; its speed is the magnitude of that vector."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A particle moves with x(t) = 2t and y(t) = t². Where is it at t = 3, and what is its average velocity vector from t = 0 to 3?","steps":["Position at t = 3: (6, 9)","Change: ⟨6 − 0, 9 − 0⟩ over 3 seconds"],"answer":"(6, 9); average velocity ⟨2, 3⟩"}]},{"say":"Matrices can transform the plane: stretching, rotating and reflecting shapes. They also model changing states.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A 2×2 matrix multiplies a vector to transform it. The determinant ad − bc of [[a, b], [c, d]] tells how areas scale: |det| is the area scale factor, and a negative determinant means orientation flips."},{"t":"key","text":"A matrix has an inverse if and only if its determinant is not zero. The inverse undoes the transformation."},{"t":"p","text":"Rotation by θ: [[cos θ, −sin θ], [sin θ, cos θ]]."},{"t":"p","text":"Transition matrices model systems that move between states, like customers switching between brands each month."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Find the determinant of [[3, 1], [4, 2]].","steps":["ad − bc = 3(2) − 1(4)"],"answer":"2 (areas double)"},{"t":"lab","kind":"demo","title":"Bike-share migration","aim":"Each day 20% of bikes at the beach move downtown and 10% of downtown bikes move to the beach. Start with all bikes downtown.","sets":[{"name":"n","start":3,"label":"Days","min":0,"max":30,"step":1},{"name":"a","start":0.2,"label":"Beach → downtown rate","min":0.05,"max":0.5,"step":0.05},{"name":"b","start":0.1,"label":"Downtown → beach rate","min":0.05,"max":0.5,"step":0.05}],"outs":[{"name":"eq","expr":"b/(a + b)","label":"Long-run share at the beach"},{"name":"beach","expr":"eq*(1 - (1 - a - b)^n)","label":"Share at the beach after n days"}],"steps":["Multiply the state vector by the transition matrix a few times. Where does it settle?"],"noise":0,"result":"Repeatedly applying the transition matrix moves the system to a steady state b/(a + b), one third of bikes at the beach here, regardless of where they started.","plot":{"x":"n","y":"beach"}}]}]];
export default text;
